  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 335 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nRidging aloft will transition across Nebraska Thursday Night, \nbreaking down Friday night into Saturday. With the ridge \nbreakdown and the approach of a cold front Saturday night, very \nwarm air will reside across western and north central Nebraska \nFriday and Saturday. Already the latest MET and MAV guidance is \nadvertising highs Friday, well into the upper 80s and lower 90s \nacross the area. The NBM is conservative with highs generally in\nthe middle to upper 80s Friday. With the ridge breakdown, and \nwell mixed southwesterly winds Friday afternoon, went ahead and \nincreased highs over the NBM initialized forecast, but under the\nMET and MAV guidance. This yielded highs in the upper 80s with \nsome readings around 90. FWIW, the latest MET and MAV guidance \nfor North Platte is 93 Friday, which would be below the record \nof 95. Records for Imperial, Broken Bow and Valentine are 91 \ndegrees Friday. Based on current forecast trends, Imperial has \nthe best chances of hitting a record high on Friday. Late \nSaturday, a cold front will approach northwestern Nebraska. Both\nthe GFS and EC solns tonight, keep the cold front northwest of \nthe forecast area through Saturday evening. Downstream of this \nfeature, another very warm day is expected across the forecast \narea. Both the statistical EC and MEX guidance tonight has highs\nreaching the upper 80s to lower 90s across the area. Like \nFriday, the NBM undercuts these numbers 3 to 5 degrees across \nthe area. For now, will utilize the NBM numbers and monitor \nforecast trends as frontal timing may be faster. As for records \nSaturday, they are 88, 93, 94 and 95 degrees for Broken Bow, \nNorth Platte, Imperial and Valentine respectively. \n\nHighs Sunday behind the cold front will be noticeably cooler with \nreadings in the middle 60s in the Pine Ridge, to the upper 70s in \nfar SW Nebraska. Later Sunday evening, the threat for precipitation \nwill increase over northwestern Nebraska as mid level forcing tracks \nfrom Wyoming into the Dakotas. Some of this forcing approaches the \nnorthwestern forecast area Sunday night into Monday. As for \nprecipitation chances, the threat for measurable pcpn. remains low \nat around 20 percent per the latest NBM ensemble probabilities. This \nis supported by the latest deterministic GFS and EC solns which \nlimit QPF to under 0.05\" over the Pine Ridge and NW Sandhills. \nMonday and Tuesday will feature cooler highs in the 60s with \nincreasing chances for precipitation Monday night into Tuesday. Flow \naloft will transition to southwesterly as a broad trough of low \npressure enters the SW CONUS.",
